steps.body:
A strategy rarely wants the volatility since the open. It wants the last 100 ticks, so yesterday's news stops counting. A rolling window keeps the last N values in a ring: a `std::array<double, N>` and an index that wraps. It never allocates, and each tick is still O(1).

While the window fills, `push` is a plain Welford step. Once it's full, the new value `x` replaces the oldest, `old`, and the count stays N. The mean moves by their difference over N, and `m2_` changes by one product:

```cpp harness
#include <iostream>

const int N = 4;
double ring_[N] = {19275.25, 19275.50, 19275.00, 19275.75};
int head_ = 0;
double mean_ = 19275.375;
double m2_ = 0.3125;

int main() {
    double x = 19276.00;
    {{code}}
    std::cout << mean_ << ' ' << m2_ / (N - 1) << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
const double old = ring_[head_];
const double old_mean = mean_;
mean_ += (x - old) / N;
m2_ += (x - old) * (x - mean_ + old - old_mean);
```

Then `x` takes `old`'s slot, and `head_` moves on, wrapping at N. In `RollingStats<N>` the size is a template parameter, so at `-O2` the `% N` becomes a multiply, or for a power of two like 64 a single `and`, the mask the `SpscQueue` from the lock-free queue lesson wrote by hand.

Each replacement leaves a little rounding error behind. Over 2 × 10^7 ticks here, the variance drifted by about 6 parts in 10^8. So `variance` clamps a tiny negative `m2_` to 0, and a long-running process recomputes `m2_` from the window every so often.

example.code:
#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <cstddef>
#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>

template <std::size_t N>
class RollingStats {
public:
    void push(double x) {
        if (count_ < N) {  // still filling: plain Welford
            ++count_;
            const double d = x - mean_;
            mean_ += d / count_;
            m2_ += d * (x - mean_);
        } else {           // full: x replaces the oldest value
            const double old = ring_[head_];
            const double old_mean = mean_;
            mean_ += (x - old) / N;
            m2_ += (x - old) * (x - mean_ + old - old_mean);
        }
        ring_[head_] = x;
        head_ = (head_ + 1) % N;
    }
    bool full() const { return count_ == N; }
    double mean() const { return mean_; }
    double variance() const { return count_ > 1 ? std::max(m2_, 0.0) / (count_ - 1) : 0.0; }
    double stdev() const { return std::sqrt(variance()); }

private:
    std::array<double, N> ring_{};
    std::size_t head_ = 0;  // the slot the next value goes in, the oldest once full
    std::size_t count_ = 0;
    double mean_ = 0.0;
    double m2_ = 0.0;
};

int main() {
    RollingStats<4> last4;
    double mid;
    std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(4);
    while (std::cin >> mid) {
        last4.push(mid);
        std::cout << mid;
        if (last4.full()) {
            std::cout << "  mean " << last4.mean() << "  sd " << last4.stdev();
        }
        std::cout << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}

